By the end of the tutorial, participants should be able to:

connect autoregressive modeling and post-training to modern LLM behavior;

compare architectures for audio, score-image, symbolic, and text inputs;

select capabilities such as instruction following, reasoning, or tool use for a musical task;

identify suitable benchmarks and limitations for a music-language system.

TODO: Add one motivating example that follows a user request from natural
language input to a musical output.

Evaluation should separate language quality from musical correctness and task
utility. A useful evaluation card records:

task and target user;

input modalities and required evidence;

automatic metrics and human judgments;

contamination and memorization risks;

uncertainty, failure modes, and subgroup analysis.

Discussion: Can a fluent answer score highly while ignoring the audio? Design
a control condition that would expose this behavior.
